{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And this is the law of the sacrifice of peace-offerings, which he shall offer to HaShem.</b> It is not said 'which he shall offer to HaShem' concerning all the offerings, but only concerning the peace-offerings, which are offered without sin. For they draw near to HaShem more than all the offerings that come on account of sin, and for this reason it is written nearby (Leviticus 7:29–30): 'He that offers the sacrifice of his peace-offerings to HaShem shall bring his offering to HaShem; his own hands shall bring the fire-offerings of HaShem.' In all of them 'to HaShem' is mentioned, for 'in these I delight, says HaShem.' And look to the right and see that concerning no offering does it mention that the owner must bring with his own hands the portion of the Most High — only concerning the peace-offerings, 'his own hands shall bring it.' For everyone who has anger from the king and wishes to atone and to placate the face of his wrath — he sends the gift before him by the hand of an agent, in the manner that Jacob said (Genesis 32:21): 'I will placate his face with the gift that goes before me, and afterward I shall see his face.' But one who brings the king a gift by way of homage and honor — he brings the gift with his own hands, in person, and not through an agent. And this is the distinction between the sin-offering and the guilt-offering, which come on account of the sin of deed, and the burnt-offering, which comes on account of the sin of thought, on the one hand, and the peace-offerings on the other. For those offerings that come to remove the face of HaShem's wrath — it is not fitting that 'his own hands shall bring it,' for it would appear as brazenness toward Heaven; therefore he sends them by the hand of the priests. But the peace-offerings, which are homage and a gift — 'his own hands shall bring it.'" "And there are those who interpret the verse 'He that offers the sacrifice of his peace-offerings to HaShem shall bring his offering to HaShem, from the sacrifice of his peace-offerings' — that it is fitting to bless him and say: May it be His will that all his offerings that he brings to HaShem shall be from the sacrifice of his peace-offerings, not sin-offerings and guilt-offerings and burnt-offerings. But concerning the burnt-offering it is said: 'This is the law of the burnt-offering; it is the burnt-offering' — meaning, it alone shall he bring, and he shall not repeat in his folly to bring a second time a burnt-offering for the sin of thought; but rather peace-offerings, when there is peace between him and his Creator." "And by way of allusion one may interpret the five occurrences of 'this is the law of' that are said in this passage, as corresponding to the five books of the Torah: one who engages in them is as though he offered these five kinds of offerings — burnt-offering, and meal-offering, and sin-offering, and guilt-offering, and peace-offerings. But the inaugural offerings were only for that time and not for all generations. And in this manner we find five 'this is the law of' in the portion of the leper, for there too our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Arakhin 15b): What is the remedy for one who spreads evil speech? If he is a Torah scholar, let him engage in Torah, etc. If so, the Torah protects from the evil speech that brings about these five kinds of leprosy, and one who engages in the five books of the Torah is saved from them, in the manner explained in the Akeidah, that in every book the punishment for the sin of the tongue is recalled. So too with these offerings — one who engages in these five books is as though he offered these five kinds of offerings." "For one who engages in the book of Genesis is as though he offered a burnt-offering. And it is 'this is the law of the burnt-offering,' for in it the law of the burnt-offering is explained, since Abel offered a burnt-offering from the firstborn of his flock (Genesis 4:4), and so too of Noah it is written: 'And he offered burnt-offerings on the altar' (Genesis 8:20), and so too Abraham: 'And he took the ram and offered it up for a burnt-offering in place of his son' (Genesis 22:13), and so too Jacob: 'And he offered sacrifices to the Elohim of his father Isaac' (Genesis 46:1)." "And one who engages in the book of Exodus is as though he offered a meal-offering. For all meal-offerings consist of unleavened bread, and in this book all the laws of unleavened bread are explained — its requirement and its combining with the paschal sacrifice, which is eaten on unleavened bread — as it is written (Exodus 34:25): 'You shall not slaughter the blood of My sacrifice upon leavened bread.'" "And one who engages with the book of Leviticus — it is as though he brought a sin-offering. Even though all the offerings are explained in this book, nevertheless the essence of the book is the law of the sin-offering, for after Israel sinned with the calf — which was the beginning of every sin and every transgression — HaShem made a place for sinners to be able to come to atonement by means of the offering, and along with this all the other offerings were mentioned, for there is in all of them an aspect of sin, except for the peace-offerings." "And one who engages with the book of Numbers — it is as though he brought a guilt-offering. For in it the entire law of the guilt-offering is stated, in the portion of Naso (Numbers 5:6): ״אִישׁ אוֹ אִשָּׁה כִּי יַעֲשׂוּ מִכָּל חַטֹּאת הָאָדָם וְאָשְׁמָה הַנֶּפֶשׁ הַהִוא״ and so forth. And even though the law of the guilt-offering was already stated in the portion of Leviticus (5:14), nevertheless it was repeated there for a matter that was newly introduced, as Rashi explained there. And if so, by means of studying the portion together with the matter that was newly introduced in it, the entire law of the guilt-offering is completed." "And one who engages with the book of Deuteronomy — it is as though he brought peace-offerings. For in the portion of Ki Tavo (27:7) it is stated: ״וְזָבַחְתָּ שְׁלָמִים וְאָכַלְתָּ שָּׁם״ and so forth. And likewise in the portion of Re'eh all the laws of peace-offerings, minor holy things, are stated, as it is written (ibid. 12:27): ״וְדַם זְבָחֶיךָ יִשָּׁפֵךְ עַל מִזְבַּח ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ וְהַבָּשָׂר תֹּאכֵל״. And there the laws of peace-offerings are completed, on account of several matters that were newly introduced concerning them. Therefore these five ״זֹאת תּוֹרַת״ were stated, for one who engages with each particular book — his reward is great, as though he brought that species of offering which is explained in that book. And those who understand will grasp for the multitudes the preciousness of this matter, for it is clear and certain."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrase כלפי שמיא ('toward Heaven') is translated literally; the idiom denotes presumption before the Divine."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:35.557031687Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}